<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satuq Bughra Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 955</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Satuq Bughra Khan was a ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the first Turkic khan known to have converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, around 934. With Samanid support he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, and his successors made Islam the religion of the state and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a>. His tomb at Artux near Kashgar remains a place of pilgrimage.</p></div>
